{:edb/db-before-t 132 :edb/db-after-t 133 :edb/tx-data [[13194139533445 50 #inst "2026-10-10T22:30:09.880Z" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186152183 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362496 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186152632 1068 #edb/ref 17592186150269 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186152632 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362490 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186152632 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186152632 1071 "Find the particular solution $y_{p}(x)$ of the equation\n\n$x^{2}\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} - 3x\\frac{dy}{dx} + 3y = 10\\sin (\\ln x), x > 0$." 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186152632 1071 "Find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ of the equation \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-3x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+3y=10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x),\\,x>0.\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186152632 1074 "This Cauchy-Euler equation has non-polynomial forcing, but we are not given two fundamental solutions, so we cannot use variation of parameters.\n\nHowever, if we make a substitution $x={e}^{t},$ we can transform the Cauchy-Euler equation into a linear ODE with constant coefficients.\n\nLetting $x={e}^{t},$ we note that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}={e}^{t}=x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we use the chain rule to compute the first and second derivatives. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}, \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\  & ={{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{x})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{product rule}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot {{\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{chain rule}}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}-4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+3y=10\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is a second-order inhomogeneous differential equation with constant coefficients and sinusoidal forcing. \n\nUsing the usual methods for solving second-order ODEs, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{t}+B{e}^{3t}.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $10\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}t}=-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}=-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation for $y(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡t)-4(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡t)+3(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡t) & =10\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ (-\\alpha -4\\beta +3\\alpha )\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+(-\\beta +4\\alpha +3\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & =10\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ (2\\alpha -4\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+(4\\alpha +2\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & =10\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2\\alpha -4\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\ 4\\alpha +2\\beta =10\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\alpha =2$ and $\\beta =1,$ and so the particular integral ${y}_{p}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nNow, returning to the variable $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x).\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186166770 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362503 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186166972 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362462 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186229196 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362483 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186231415 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362469 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186231415 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362476 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362462 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362463 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362462 1069 "q-344852" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362462 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362462 1071 "$$\n{y}^{‴}-2{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}-8y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\\,{{y}_{1}={e}^{2x},\\,{y}_{2}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),\\,{y}_{3}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation and the set of fundamental solutions of the corresponding homogeneous equation above. \nUsing the method of variation of parameters, the general solution of the equation can be written in the form\n\n$$\ny(x)=A{e}^{2x}+B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+C\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+{u}_{1}(x){e}^{2x}+{u}_{2}(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+{u}_{3}(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nWhat is the function ${u}_{2}(x)?$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362462 1072 #uuid "abe3ca3c-2b3b-553e-adf2-1832b16d6f75" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362462 1074 "According to the method of variation of parameters, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2}+{u}_{3}{y}_{3},\\,{u}_{i}=(-1)^{3-i}\\int \\frac{g(x){W}_{i}(x)}{W(x)}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,i=1,2,3,\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is the forcing term, $W(x)$ is the Wronskian of ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}_{1}(x)=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{aligned}|,\\,{W}_{2}(x)=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{aligned}|,\\,{W}_{3}(x)=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the forcing term is $g(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}={e}^{2x},\\,{y}_{2}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),\\,{y}_{3}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1},$ ${y}_{2},$ and ${y}_{3}$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(x) & =W({y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3}) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2x} & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) \\\\ 2{e}^{2x} & -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) & 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) \\\\ 4{e}^{2x} & -4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) \\\\ 2 & -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) & 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) \\\\ 4 & -4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}[(8{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)+8{cos}^{2}⁡(2x))-2(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))+4(2{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)+2{cos}^{2}⁡(2x))] \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}[8-2(0)+4(2)] \\\\  & =16{e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1},$ ${y}_{2},$ and ${y}_{3}$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, to find ${u}_{2},$ we compute the determinant ${W}_{2}(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}_{2}(x) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2x} & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) \\\\ 2{e}^{2x} & 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-2{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) \\\\  & =2{e}^{2x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2}(x) & =(-1)^{3-2}\\int \\frac{g(x){W}_{2}(x)}{W(x)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))\\cdot 2{e}^{2x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))}{16{e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}\\int [{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)-{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{32}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362463 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362464 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362463 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362465 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362463 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362466 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362463 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362467 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362463 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362468 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362463 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362463 1077 "selection" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362463 1079 #edb/ref 17592186362464 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362463 1080 #uuid "e14a1966-6c75-5e57-a9d6-80b7e25a7cbe" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362464 1081 "-\\frac{1}{32}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362464 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362464 1084 #uuid "c61b6982-5bd8-533d-86f1-27f43e8933c0" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362465 1081 "-\\frac{1}{16}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362465 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362465 1084 #uuid "fcf8ae02-44ac-5979-abbc-15eb3fa823e2" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362466 1081 "\\frac{1}{32}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362466 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362466 1084 #uuid "13d98f74-ef79-5149-84ce-ce3995f69eb5" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362467 1081 "\\frac{1}{32}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362467 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362467 1084 #uuid "8d6b8795-5fdf-5a09-88b8-3ac05adf4a7e" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362468 1081 "-\\frac{1}{16}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362468 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362468 1084 #uuid "f7592b21-a147-5e11-bf33-b28810d18efd" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362469 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362470 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362469 1069 "q-345394" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362469 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362469 1071 "![](images/53/5322f43ff1eeee6e06b3c393cc1b59c9af32bc2e7dba66766607af117954ace1.png)\n\nFind, in amperes, the amplitude of the steady state current for the RCL circuit shown above.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362469 1072 #uuid "87500776-9edf-567b-97b4-f626c972de46" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362469 1074 "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $20\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.1\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $10\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=20\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=20I.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.1}=10q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=10\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=10\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, these values are related by the equation\n\n$$\n8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)=20I+10q+10\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $t,$ the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}(8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(20I+10q+10\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}) \\\\ -8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & =20\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+10\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+10\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in $I=\\frac{dq}{dt},$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+I=-\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}{I}_{c}}{d{t}^{2}}+2\\,\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as a trial solution, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t}+Bt{e}^{-t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants.\n\n- Next, we find a particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute this, as well as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt} & =-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t), \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}{I}_{p}}{d{t}^{2}} & =-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))+2(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)) \\\\ +(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)) & =-\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ (2\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+(-2\\alpha )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & =-\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)) \\\\ -2\\alpha =-\\frac{4}{5}\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{2}{5}$ and $\\beta =0.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t}+Bt{e}^{-t}+\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t}+Bt{e}^{-t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)≈{I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current, in amperes, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{2}+{0}^{2}}=\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362470 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362471 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362470 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362472 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362470 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362473 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362470 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362474 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362470 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362475 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362470 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362470 1077 "selection" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362470 1079 #edb/ref 17592186362474 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362470 1080 #uuid "260e3261-b455-5010-b222-cd576fad3821" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362471 1081 "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362471 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362471 1084 #uuid "27c46d96-7118-5fd9-a987-dacbb85787b0" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362472 1081 "\\frac{4}{5}" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362472 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362472 1084 #uuid "469b4611-ac86-5465-b365-22bbfa82a107" 13194139533445 17592186045585 