um of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=4\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=4I.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.25}=4q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=1\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, these values are related by the equation\n\n$$\n13\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)=4I+4q+\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $t,$ the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}(13\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(4I+4q+\\frac{dI}{dt}) \\\\ 39\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) & =4\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+4\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in $I=\\frac{dq}{dt},$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+4\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+4I=39\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}{I}_{c}}{d{t}^{2}}+4\\,\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+4{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as a trial solution, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-2t}+Bt{e}^{-2t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants.\n\n- Next, we find a particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $3t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $3t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute this, as well as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt} & =-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t), \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}{I}_{p}}{d{t}^{2}} & =-9\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-9\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-9\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-9\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))+4(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)) \\\\ +4(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =39\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ (-5\\alpha +12\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+(-12\\alpha -5\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & =39\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-5\\alpha +12\\beta =39\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)) \\\\ -12\\alpha -5\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{15}{13}$ and $\\beta =\\frac{36}{13}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{15}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{36}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-2t}+Bt{e}^{-2t}-\\frac{15}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{36}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-2t}+Bt{e}^{-2t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{15}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{36}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)≈{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{15}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{36}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current, in amperes, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{15}{13})}^{2}+{(\\frac{36}{13})}^{2}}=3.\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362477 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362478 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362477 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362479 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362477 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362480 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362477 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362481 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362477 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362482 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362477 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362477 1077 "selection" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362477 1079 #edb/ref 17592186362479 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362477 1080 #uuid "451c975f-1f1e-5645-aa6b-851c1bdb0a4f" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362478 1081 "\\frac{15}{13}" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362478 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362478 1084 #uuid "9bc2a4da-03ce-57e3-9b70-0befb4d403ca" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362479 1081 "3" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362479 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362479 1084 #uuid "b28b624a-4185-5a97-a3c0-80852dfb8b8f" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362480 1081 "\\sqrt{3}" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362480 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362480 1084 #uuid "0071c68e-e3f7-5dd2-894d-a48eb8c00d96" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362481 1081 "6" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362481 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362481 