ion ${y}_{p}(x)$ of the equation \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-3x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+3y=9\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,\\,x>0.\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362496 1072 #uuid "b80a86d6-1469-5372-8f54-649c11e077fd" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362496 1074 "This Cauchy-Euler equation has non-polynomial forcing, but we are not given two fundamental solutions, so we cannot use variation of parameters.\n\nHowever, if we make a substitution $x={e}^{t},$ we can transform the Cauchy-Euler equation into a linear ODE with constant coefficients.\n\nLetting $x={e}^{t},$ we note that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}={e}^{t}=x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we use the chain rule to compute the first and second derivatives. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}, \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\  & ={{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{x})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{product rule}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot {{\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{chain rule}}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}-4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+3y=9t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is a second-order inhomogeneous differential equation with constant coefficients and polynomial forcing. \n\nUsing the usual methods for solving second-order ODEs, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{t}+B{e}^{3t}.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\alpha t+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}t}=\\alpha ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation for $y(t):$\n\n$$\n0-4\\alpha +3(\\alpha t+\\beta )=9t\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n3\\alpha t+(3\\beta -4\\alpha )=9t.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}3\\alpha =9\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of }t) \\\\ 3\\beta -4\\alpha =0\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\alpha =3$ and $\\beta =4,$ and so the particular integral ${y}_{p}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=3t+4.\n$$\n\nNow, returning to the variable $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+4.\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362497 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362498 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362497 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362497 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362500 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362497 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362501 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362497 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362502 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362497 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362497 1077 "selection" 1319413953344